WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.656
Cargo can use public registries, private
registries, alternate sources,

2
00:00:03.736 --> 00:00:05.941
and organization-specific publishing
workflows.

3
00:00:06.021 --> 00:00:09.478
Registry and source configuration affect
where dependencies come from and which

4
00:00:09.558 --> 00:00:11.211
packages a build is allowed to resolve.

5
00:00:11.291 --> 00:00:13.696
That configuration should be intentional,
documented,

6
00:00:13.776 --> 00:00:15.679
and consistent with organizational policy.

7
00:00:15.759 --> 00:00:18.014
Publishing authority is a security
responsibility.

8
00:00:18.094 --> 00:00:21.400
A package release can affect every
downstream consumer that trusts the crate.

9
00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:23.434
Teams should know who owns publishing
rights,

10
00:00:23.514 --> 00:00:26.520
which accounts or automation can publish,
how release approval works,

11
00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:28.304
and how ownership changes are reviewed.

12
00:00:28.384 --> 00:00:31.790
Release authority should not be a vague
privilege attached to whoever happens to

13
00:00:31.870 --> 00:00:32.622
have an old token.

14
00:00:32.702 --> 00:00:35.257
Registry tokens and publishing credentials
are sensitive.

15
00:00:35.337 --> 00:00:38.493
They may authorize package release actions
or access to private packages.

16
00:00:38.573 --> 00:00:41.128
They should not live in source
repositories, shell history,

17
00:00:41.208 --> 00:00:43.914
general-purpose logs, developer notes,
shared chat messages,

18
00:00:43.994 --> 00:00:45.096
or broad CI/CD variables.

19
00:00:45.176 --> 00:00:47.631
They should be scoped, stored in approved
secret systems,

20
00:00:47.711 --> 00:00:50.316
rotated when appropriate, and revoked when
no longer needed.

21
00:00:50.396 --> 00:00:53.602
CI/CD publishing workflows need separation
between build, test, package,

22
00:00:53.682 --> 00:00:54.484
and release steps.

23
00:00:54.564 --> 00:00:58.071
A pipeline that can build code does not
always need permission to publish packages.

24
00:00:58.151 --> 00:01:01.758
A release job that holds publishing
authority should run under stricter conditions,

25
00:01:01.838 --> 00:01:03.692
with reviewed inputs and clear provenance.

26
00:01:03.772 --> 00:01:07.279
This reduces the chance that an ordinary
build path accidentally becomes a release

27
00:01:07.359 --> 00:01:07.609
path.

28
00:01:07.689 --> 00:01:10.244
Publishing should be deliberate, reviewed,
and traceable.

29
00:01:10.324 --> 00:01:13.731
Versioning, changelogs, source commit,
dependency state, build environment,

30
00:01:13.811 --> 00:01:17.368
artifact handling, and rollback
expectations should be part of release readiness.

31
00:01:17.448 --> 00:01:18.901
The goal is not heavy bureaucracy.

32
00:01:18.981 --> 00:01:22.488
The goal is that a published crate or
production artifact can be explained after the

33
00:01:22.568 --> 00:01:23.620
fact without guesswork.
